<article class="env-lemma" id="0CX8">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CX8">Lemma <span data-tag="0CX8">77.12.5</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/0CX4" data-tag="0CX4">77.12.1</a> let $K$ be as in Lemma <a href="/tag/0CX5" data-tag="0CX5">77.12.2</a>. For any étale morphism $U \to X$ with $U$ quasi-compact and quasi-separated we have </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  R\Gamma (U, K) \otimes _ A^\mathbf {L} A_ n = R\Gamma (U_ n, \mathcal{F}_ n)  \]
</div>
  <p> in $D(A_ n)$ where $U_ n = U \times _ X X_ n$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Fix $n$. By Derived Categories of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/0CRW" data-tag="0CRW">75.27.3</a> there exists a system of perfect complexes $E_ m$ on $X$ such that $R\Gamma (U, K) = \text{hocolim} R\Gamma (X, K \otimes ^\mathbf {L} E_ m)$. In fact, this formula holds not just for $K$ but for every object of $D_\mathit{QCoh}(\mathcal{O}_ X)$. Applying this to $\mathcal{F}_ n$ we obtain 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \begin{align*}  R\Gamma (U_ n, \mathcal{F}_ n) &  = R\Gamma (U, \mathcal{F}_ n) \\ &  = \text{hocolim}_ m R\Gamma (X, \mathcal{F}_ n \otimes ^\mathbf {L} E_ m) \\ &  = \text{hocolim}_ m R\Gamma (X_ n, \mathcal{F}_ n \otimes ^\mathbf {L} E_ m|_{X_ n}) \end{align*}
</div>
    <p>
       Using Lemma <a href="/tag/0CX6" data-tag="0CX6">77.12.3</a> and the fact that $- \otimes _ A^\mathbf {L} A_ n$ commutes with homotopy colimits we obtain the result. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>